  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "note-sheet",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 400,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "The scoreboard sheet\n\nRound two, on the sheet. What changed from round one: it is a stop in the column and its answer says where you stand (your side and its score, or Watching); a side's row is now a defined thing in the menu language, the selectable section, drawn in three shapes with the ring leading; every pilot row carries a Time; and each zone is drawn on the next page, the duel with its rounds.\n\nOn naming: SCOREBOARD leads because its answer is where you stand in every zone, the duel and Free Roam included. TEAMS is drawn beside it on the Team Battle board to compare.\n\nThe stop is a fifth one. Decision 143 named the fifth stop as the number to watch on a short window's rail; that cost is real and drawn on the phone column."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-zones",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 400,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "By zone\n\nOne sheet, five zones. Turf and War put each side's stands or flags on its row, in the side's color, with the ones nobody holds dim. The duel's sides are single pilots, so the section is the pilot, with a Rounds band under them listing who took each and how long it ran. Free Roam is eight sides of eight with no clock and no score: the rings say which you could join, and the list scrolls under the pinned head."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-band",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 400,
      "page": "page-3",
      "text": "The band\n\nThe sheet hangs off an instrument, and the instrument can be one of three: the shipped 26-point band at top center, a 16-point line in the same place, or a stack in the top left corner. The big sheet draws all three for every zone; the boards under it put each in a Turf room on a monitor and a War room on a phone, with the corner band once with the sheet up.\n\nMiddle keeps the first glance. Thin spends less of the top edge and makes the clock small. Corner clears the top edge entirely and sits where the room's chips come and go."
    }
  ],
